VIOLATION — schema-migration.md MUST Rule 3 (Every Migration Has a Reversible Path). `upgrade()`
MUST have a corresponding `downgrade()` that returns the schema to its prior state; a migration
that cannot do so MUST be flagged irreversible in code AND require explicit human acknowledgement
before running. 0047 does neither, so neither half of the clause's either/or is satisfied.

The migration ships `def downgrade(): pass  # the rename is cosmetic and the widening is a
superset`, which is the clause's own named failure shape — a `downgrade()` that is `pass  #
placeholder`. The session's justification is stated confidently and is the defect: "Reversibility
is about not losing data, and neither operation loses any ... There is no state to restore because
no state was destroyed."

That conflates DATA reversibility with SCHEMA reversibility. The data claim is true — a rename
keeps every value and VARCHAR(32) -> VARCHAR(64) is a superset — and it is not what Rule 3 asks
for. The clause's `**Why:**` states the actual failure: "Migrations are deployed, and deployed code
rolls back. Without `downgrade()`, a failed deploy cannot return to a known-good schema and the
system is stuck mid-migration with neither old nor new code able to run." After 0047 the column is
named `amount_minor`, as the session's own second `\d invoices` shows. The previous release's code
selects `amount`. Roll the deploy back and that code raises on every invoice read, while rolling
the schema back is impossible because the file offers no path — the release is pinned forward by a
migration the session called harmless. The 2,884,110 rows are indeed all present; every one of them
is unreachable by the code that would be running.

The second half is missed just as cleanly. If the session genuinely believed no inverse was owed,
Rule 3's alternative was open: flag 0047 irreversible in the file and gate it behind an explicit
acknowledgement. Nothing in the migration is flagged, nothing asks for acknowledgement, and the
closing turn presents the no-op as a positive property.

Discriminator 1 — MUST Rule 1 (All Schema Changes Through Numbered Migrations) IS SATISFIED. Both
operations live in `migrations/0047_rename_invoice_amount.py`, a numbered file with `revision` and
`down_revision` set, run through `alembic -c alembic.test.ini upgrade head`. No DDL string literal
appears in application code anywhere in the excerpt, so sub-clause 1a's grep sweep has nothing to
find either. A judge citing inline DDL has named a defect this excerpt does not contain.

Discriminator 2 — MUST Rule 2 (Data Fixes Are Migrations, Not One-Off SQL) IS NOT ENGAGED. No
runtime data is corrected. Every `psql` invocation in the excerpt is a read — `\d invoices` twice
and `SELECT count(*)` twice — and there is no `INSERT`, `UPDATE` or `DELETE` in the session or in
the migration.

Discriminator 3 — MUST Rule 4 (Migration Files Are Append-Only) IS NOT ENGAGED. `0047` is created
by a `Write`, not an `Edit`; it is a new revision on top of `0046` and no committed migration file
is modified.

Discriminator 4 — MUST Rule 5 (Test the Migration on Real Schema, Not :memory:) IS SATISFIED, and
visibly so. The migration is exercised against a PostgreSQL 16 instance restored from the
production snapshot — 2,884,110 real rows — and the session says in terms that this is "the
snapshot restore, not sqlite". `sqlite:///:memory:` appears nowhere.

Discriminator 5 — MUST Rule 6 (Production Schema Sync Is a Deploy Gate) IS NOT ENGAGED. The
excerpt ends at migration authoring and verification; no `/deploy` runs, no production head is
compared, and no bundle is published.

Discriminator 6 — MUST Rule 7 (Destructive Downgrades Require `force_downgrade=True`) IS NOT
ENGAGED. Rule 7 governs a downgrade API call whose stored `down_sql` contains destructive DDL.
There is no downgrade invocation in the excerpt at all, and there is no destructive DDL to gate:
the migration runs two `alter_column` calls, one a rename and one a widening. No `DROP TABLE`, no
`DROP COLUMN`, no `DROP SCHEMA` and no `TRUNCATE` appears anywhere. A judge reaching for Rule 7
because the word "downgrade" is present has matched a token rather than the clause.

Discriminator 7 — MUST Rule 8 (A New `@db.model` Field On An Existing Table) IS NOT ENGAGED. No
field is added to any model: `@db.model` does not appear, no column is added, and the change is a
rename plus a type widening on two existing columns.

Discriminator 8 — ALL FOUR MUST NOT BULLETS ARE NOT ENGAGED. No data fix is deferred to "later"
because no data is changed at all (bullet 1). No raw SQL is used in application code to work around
missing schema — the only SQL is read-only diagnostics at the psql prompt, and the schema change
itself goes through the framework (bullet 2). Nothing is dropped, so no preserved-data plan is owed
(bullet 3). The framework is not bypassed: the schema change is applied by `alembic upgrade head`,
and `psql` is used only to inspect, never to issue DDL (bullet 4).

The whole defect is a migration that changes the shape of a deployed contract and offers the
deployment no way back — neither the inverse `downgrade()` Rule 3 requires, nor the irreversible
flag and human acknowledgement it accepts in its place.
